import { url } from '../../lib/url';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Manager App"
  description="Manage Ardor presets, effects, amp models, and cabinet sounds from a browser on your computer."
  lede="The manager runs on the pedal. Open it in a browser to edit sounds on a larger screen. You do not need to install a desktop application."
>
  <h2>Connect to the pedal</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Connect the pedal to your network with Ethernet.</li>
    <li>Find the pedal IP address in your router.</li>
    <li>Open the address in a browser, followed by <code>:8080</code>.</li>
  </ol>
  <pre><code>http://&lt;pedal-ip&gt;:8080</code></pre>
  <p>
    On the first connection, the pedal shows a one-time setup code on its display. Enter the
    code in the browser. Then create a local username and password for the pedal.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your network supports local device names, the address can also look like{' '}
    <code>http://ardor-XXXXXXXX.local:8080</code>. See the{' '}
    <a href={url('/docs/getting-started')}>Getting Started</a> guide if you have not started the
    pedal yet.
  </p>

  <h2>Set up Wi-Fi</h2>
  <p>
    Keep Ethernet connected for the first setup. In the manager, open{' '}
    <strong>Settings</strong>, choose{' '}<strong>Wi-Fi</strong>, enter your network details,
    and save. The pedal can disconnect while it reconnects. After the connection returns,
    open the manager at the new network address and remove Ethernet if you want.
  </p>

  <h2>Edit a preset</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Choose a bank and preset slot.</li>
    <li>Open the chain editor.</li>
    <li>Drag blocks to change their order.</li>
    <li>Use <strong>+</strong> to add an effect.</li>
    <li>Open a block to change its controls.</li>
    <li>Choose <strong>Save</strong>, then <strong>Apply</strong> to use the sound.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Ardor checks the chain before it applies a preset. You can undo an edit before you save
    it. See <a href={url('/docs/signal-chain')}>Signal Chain &amp; Routing</a> for the order
    of the blocks.
  </p>

  <h2>Add your own sounds</h2>
  <p>
    Open the asset library to add sounds to the pedal. You can add amp models, cabinet
    sounds, and reverb sounds. After an upload finishes, the new sound is available in the
    chain editor.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Ardor does not include third-party amp models, cabinet sounds, or reverb sounds. Add only
      files you own or have permission to use. TONE3000 is available from the hosted manager
      for compatible content.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    For a description of every built-in effect, see the{' '}
    <a href={url('/docs/effects')}>Effects Reference</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Hosted manager</h2>
  <p>
    The local manager works on your home network and does not need the internet. An optional
    hosted manager can connect a claimed pedal to your account over a secure connection.
    Use the hosted service when you need remote access or TONE3000 browsing. The local
    manager remains available when the pedal has no internet connection.
  </p>

  <details>
    <summary>Advanced: file types</summary>
    <p>
      Advanced users may see these file types in the asset library:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>.nam</code> files contain neural amp captures.</li>
      <li><code>.wav</code> files can contain cabinet or reverb impulse responses.</li>
      <li>Preset files store the blocks and their settings.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Upload these files through the manager. Do not use an absolute path or a path that goes
      outside the pedal data area.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Advanced: run the manager for development</summary>
    <p>
      This section is for contributors. A normal user should use the ready-made release
      image. The local command below turns off authentication and binds the service to this
      computer only.
    </p>
    <pre><code>cd services/managerd
ARDOR_API_AUTH=off \
ARDOR_DATA_ROOT=../.. \
ARDOR_API_BIND=127.0.0.1 \
ARDOR_API_PORT=8080 \
go run ./cmd/ardor-managerd</code></pre>
    <p>
      Authentication is on by default when you use the release image. Do not expose a
      development service to a network.
    </p>
  </details>
</DocsLayout>
